---
Generic building blocks that know nothing about tickets. Each type
parameter carries exactly the bound its body needs, so the signature tells
a caller what it must supply.
---
commons triage.generic

uses bynk.list

---
How many times `x` appears in `xs`. Comparing with `==` is the only thing
this does to a `T`, so `T: Eq` is the whole requirement.
---
fn occurrences[T: Eq](xs: List[T], x: T) -> Int {
	xs.filter((y) => y == x).length()
}

---
Pins `x` between `lo` and `hi`. `<` and `>` need an order, so `T: Ord`.
`Ord` implies `Eq`, so there is no need to write both.
---
fn clamp[T: Ord](x: T, lo: T, hi: T) -> T {
	if x < lo { lo } else { if x > hi { hi } else { x } }
}

---
Counts the items of `xs` under the key `key` gives each one. `K` keys a
`Map`, so it is `Key`; `V` is only passed to `key`, so it stays unbounded
and any type will do.
---
fn countBy[K: Key, V](xs: List[V], key: V -> K) -> Map[K, Int] {
	let init: Map[K, Int] = Map.empty()
	xs.fold(init, (acc, x) => {
		let k = key(x)
		let seen = match acc.get(k) {
			Some(n) => n,
			None => 0,
		}
		acc.insert(k, seen + 1)
	})
}

---
A list kept in ascending order. The bound sits on the type, so every
method sees `T: Ord` without restating it. Build one through the statics
below, which live under the type's own name: each one returns its items in
order. `items` is still a public field, so a hand-written `Sorted { … }`
literal can skip the sort: only a record can be generic, and an opaque type,
which would hide the field, cannot.
---
type Sorted[T: Ord] = {
	items: List[T],
}

--- Sorts `xs` once, on the way in. `T` is inferred from the argument.
fn Sorted.fromList(xs: List[T]) -> Sorted[T] {
	Sorted { items: sort(xs) }
}

---
An empty `Sorted`. With no argument to infer `T` from, the caller names it
(`Sorted.empty[Int]()`) or gives an expected type.
---
fn Sorted.empty() -> Sorted[T] {
	Sorted { items: [] }
}

---
A copy with `x` added in its place. Re-sorting keeps this short. An ordered
insert into the already-sorted list would be O(n) rather than O(n log n).
---
fn Sorted.insert(self, x: T) -> Sorted[T] {
	Sorted { items: sort(self.items.prepend(x)) }
}

--- The smallest item, or `None` when empty.
fn Sorted.first(self) -> Option[T] {
	self.items.get(0)
}

---
Who a ticket goes to, then who after them. The type refers to itself through
`next`, so a chain can be any length, and it is generic in what each step
holds. A recursive generic type is ordinary inside a unit; it is refused only
where a value would cross a boundary (a handler signature, a `store` field, an
event), because its codec doesn't exist yet
(`bynk.generics.recursive_generic_at_boundary`).
---
type Chain[T] = {
	head: T,
	next: Option[Chain[T]],
}

--- How many steps the chain has, at least one.
fn Chain.length(self) -> Int {
	stepsFrom(self, 1)
}

---
Counts on from `n`. The self call is the last thing each step does, a tail
call, so it runs as a loop: a long chain doesn't grow the stack.
---
fn stepsFrom[T](c: Chain[T], n: Int) -> Int {
	match c.next {
		Some(rest) => stepsFrom(rest, n + 1),
		None => n,
	}
}

--- The step a ticket ends up with once every earlier one has passed it on.
fn Chain.last(self) -> T {
	match self.next {
		Some(rest) => rest.last(),
		None => self.head,
	}
}
